#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/id #uuid "b0a1a305-4617-50f6-a0d9-2b573277f68f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21883" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2 \\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $X$, what is the third principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\lambda_3\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then:\n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$,\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$,\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue $\\lambda_3$.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= \\frac{1}{n - 1}X^{T}X \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}(UΣV^{T})^{T}(UΣV^{T}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}VΣ^{T}(U^{T}U)ΣV^{T} \\\\\n&= V(\\frac{1}{n - 1}Σ^{T}Σ)V^{T}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C$. Notice the following:\n\n- The matrix $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ is a diagonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$.\n- The matrix $V$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$.\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/id #uuid "ad59f3c2-8ca8-5d05-b626-501682800f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-21884" :question/problem "A financial analyst measured $5$ features, $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_5$, for each company among a group of companies under study. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X$, where each row contains measurements for a particular company.\n\nThe singular values of $X$ are\n$\\sigma_1=7, \\quad \\sigma_2=5, \\quad \\sigma_3=3, \\quad \\sigma_4=2, \\quad \\sigma_5=1$.\nThe analyst then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y = X V_2$, where $V_2$ is the $5 \\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY = XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are uncorrelated),\n- the features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n- the squares of the singular values $\\sigma_1^2, \\sigma_2^2, \\ldots, \\sigma_5^2$ represent the variances in the respective features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$.\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\nTotal \\mid ∝∑_(i = 1)^(5)σ_{i}^{2}; = (7)^{2} + (5)^{2} + (3)^{2} + (2)^{2} + (1)^{2}; = 88\n$$\n\nSince the analyst used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\nRemaining \\mid ∝∑_(i = 1)^(2)σ_{i}^{2}; = (7)^{2} + (5)^{2}; = 74\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{Remaining}{Total} &= \\frac{74}{88} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.841 \\\\\n&= 84.1%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5228a68-1e0a-5761-ac90-40fcab271214" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92fddb6b-8b7d-5b71-98e3-e3db17167e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191812} {:answer/id #uuid "2be2018c-97b2-5cda-a186-67bf178440d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191813} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3a1841-a0f7-5812-a954-3cbe2e902eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191814} {:answer/id #uuid "46f39583-3f0d-5c5d-9c57-b969de530c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191815} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7369b9-68bc-5161-a71b-484d48dc7289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191816}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7a2b67-b371-5a62-947f-c82e0ac64156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191811}] :question/id #uuid "2a712975-c967-5929-93fa-93c38ef8445c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343711" :question/problem "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2 \\cdot 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X$, what is the second principal component of the data?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7d70fa-bf88-5d8a-b589-211fc1508e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191824} {:answer/id #uuid "f22fa831-0ffa-5b44-8d4d-6444a734e999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191825} {:answer/id #uuid "229c730c-c45a-5588-82ae-49b26c273ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} {:answer/id #uuid "41964a93-85b9-57dd-b859-9b726749f309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191827} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4abaff-9204-5ea0-a799-46b24af32d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191828}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0c783572-57af-5f59-b3ff-cca2d8bf3bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191823}] :question/id #uuid "edd41528-ab75-576f-8bc0-8fe5a9ab1f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-343713" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{5}$, for each specimen among a group of animals in danger of extinction. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X$, where each row contains measurements for a particular specimen.\nThe singular values of $X$ are\n$σ_{1} = 7, σ_{2} = 2, σ_{3} = 1, σ_{4} = 0.6, σ_{5} = 0.4$.\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y = XV_{1}$, where $V_{1}$ is the $5 \\cdot 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
